.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt DTFL_DDIR_EXPLAIN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_ddir_explain ,
.Nm dtfl_ddir_clone_identity
.Nd rewrite a DDIR program into one that explains its output
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/ddir.h
.Ft int
.Fn dtfl_ddir_explain "const dtfl_ddir_program *program" "const dtfl_ddir_shape *shapes" "size_t n_shapes" "dtfl_ddir_program **out"
.Ft int
.Fn dtfl_ddir_clone_identity "const dtfl_ddir_program *program" "dtfl_ddir_program **out"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dtfl_ddir_explain
rewrites
.Fa program
into a new program whose execution produces, for a query against
.Fa program Ns 's
first export, the per-source
.Em demand sets :
the input rows that regenerate the queried output row.
.Pp
.Fa shapes
gives the
.Vt dtfl_ddir_shape
.Pq a Fa k No key and Fa v No value field count
of each of the program's positional inputs, in order;
.Fa n_shapes
must equal
.Fn dtfl_ddir_program_input_count .
The shapes let the rewrite place the loop-iteration chain in the flat
demand envelope that carries each query.
.Pp
The rewritten program takes one extra positional input after the
originals.
A row of that input is a query: its key is the queried output row's key,
and its value is the queried value's fields followed by a trailing query
id.
The rewritten program exports one collection named
.Dq Li demand:input Ns Ar N
per source
.Ar N ,
holding that source's demand set.
Restricting the original inputs to the demand sets and re-running
.Fa program
regenerates every queried output row.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_ddir_clone_identity
clones
.Fa program
into an equivalent program that computes the same named exports.
It is the identity transform the explanation rewrite is built on: every
nested scope additionally exports a host-visible lifted form of its
internal collections, folding that scope's iteration coordinates into the
value so a later pass can address any value-producing site at the top
level.
.Pp
Both write the new program through
.Fa out
and return 0 on success.
The caller owns the result and frees it with
.Fn dtfl_ddir_program_free .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each returns 0 on success.
On a NULL argument, a shape count that does not match the program's input
count, or a program with no export, each returns a
.Dv DTFL_E Ns Sy *
code, reports a message through
.Xr dtfl_last_error_detail 3 ,
and leaves
.Fa *out
NULL.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_ddir_parse 3
.Sh STANDARDS
The explanation rewrite is a port of the
.Pa interactive/src/explain
module of differential dataflow.
.Sh CAVEATS
The reverse-tracing rules target flat relational programs: tuples of
scalar fields, keyed and reshaped by the DDIR projection language.
A demand set is an over-approximation; it is sufficient to regenerate the
queried row but need not be minimal.
